// Events marked on the charts (chart: the line they sit on), each with its RBI, Government of India or PIB source (not on DBIE).
export const EVENTS : { chart : "usd" | "neer" | "rel"; month : string; label : string; note : string; source : string; url : string }[] = [{"chart":"usd","month":"2007-04","label":"Capital inflows","note":"Large capital inflows meant foreign exchange came in faster than it was needed, and the rupee strengthened against the dollar: from ₹44.03 a dollar in March 2007 to about ₹39.4 by late 2007.","source":"Economic Survey 2007-08, Chapter 6 (External Sector), Ministry of Finance, February 2008","url":"https://www.indiabudget.gov.in/budget_archive/es2007-08/chapt2008/chap611.pdf"},{"chart":"usd","month":"2008-10","label":"Global financial crisis","note":"After Lehman Brothers failed in mid-September 2008, foreign investors withdrew funds from Indian equity markets and capital flowed out in September and October. A dollar went from about ₹40 in April 2008 to ₹51.23 in March 2009.","source":"RBI Annual Report 2008-09, Chapter II: Economic Review, 27-08-2009","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/AnnualReportPublications.aspx?Id=896"},{"chart":"usd","month":"2011-09","label":"Euro area debt crisis","note":"The US credit-rating downgrade and the deepening euro area debt crisis sent investors to the US dollar. Between August and mid-December 2011 the rupee moved by about 17% against the dollar, and again in May 2012 amid concern that Greece might leave the euro.","source":"RBI Annual Report 2011-12, Chapter II: Economic Review, para II.6.19, 23-08-2012","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/AnnualReportPublications.aspx?Id=1039"},{"chart":"usd","month":"2013-08","label":"US Fed taper signal","note":"After the US Federal Reserve signalled in May 2013 that it might slow its asset purchases, capital left emerging markets. A dollar reached ₹68.36 on 28-08-2013 (RBI reference rate); the rupee recovered after Reserve Bank and Government measures, including a swap window for FCNR(B) deposits from 04-09-2013.","source":"RBI Annual Report 2013-14, Chapter II: Economic Review, para II.4.11, 21-08-2014","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/AnnualReportPublications.aspx?Id=1120"},{"chart":"usd","month":"2020-03","label":"COVID-19 pandemic","note":"The pandemic changed sentiment abruptly in March 2020, with large outflows from Indian equity and debt markets. A dollar went from ₹71.49 in February 2020 to ₹76.24 in April.","source":"RBI Annual Report 2019-20, Chapter II: Economic Review, 25-08-2020","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/AnnualReportPublications.aspx?Id=1286"},{"chart":"usd","month":"2022-03","label":"War and US rate rises","note":"Geopolitical tensions in Eastern Europe from late February 2022, high crude oil prices, portfolio outflows and the US Federal Reserve's rate rises all bore on the rupee. A dollar went from ₹75.00 in February 2022 to about ₹82 by October.","source":"RBI Annual Report 2022-23, Chapter II: Economic Review, para II.5.32, 30-05-2023","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/AnnualReportPublications.aspx?Id=1373"},{"chart":"usd","month":"2025-08","label":"US tariffs on Indian exports","note":"From mid-July 2025 the rupee moved between ₹85.8 and ₹88.76 a dollar, with portfolio outflows, higher US tariffs on Indian exports and narrower interest-rate differentials. The monthly average passed ₹90 in December 2025.","source":"RBI Monetary Policy Report, October 2025, Chapter I (RBI Bulletin, 20-10-2025)","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_ViewBulletin.aspx?Id=23707"},{"chart":"usd","month":"2026-03","label":"West Asia conflict","note":"Conflict in the Middle East from March 2026 and the jump in crude oil prices, with portfolio outflows and a stronger dollar, bore on the rupee; 2025-26 closed at ₹94.83 a dollar. July 2026 had the highest monthly average of the series, ₹95.82.","source":"RBI Annual Report 2025-26, Chapter II: Economic Review, para II.5.22, 29-05-2026","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/AnnualReportPublications.aspx?Id=1462"},{"chart":"neer","month":"2014-11","label":"The lines cross: a strong dollar","note":"From here the dollars that were worth ₹100 in April 2004 cost more than the basket. In 2014-15 the US dollar rose 23% against other major currencies, on a robust US recovery, while weak recoveries in the euro zone and Japan pulled the euro and the yen down against it.","source":"RBI Annual Report 2014-15, Chapter II: Economic Review, para II.4.2, 27-08-2015","url":"https://rbi.org.in/Scripts/AnnualReportPublications.aspx?Id=1148"},{"chart":"rel","month":"2010-02","label":"Food prices lift inflation","note":"Inflation in India stayed in double digits from February 2010. It began with food prices after a deficient monsoon and then spread more widely. (Measured then by wholesale prices.)","source":"RBI Bulletin, 'VI. Price Situation', para VI.1, 11-08-2010","url":"https://rbi.org.in/SCRIPTs/BS_ViewBulletin.aspx?Id=11436"},{"chart":"rel","month":"2013-11","label":"Inflation peaks at 11.5%","note":"After 2008, high inflation persisted alongside weaker growth. It peaked at 11.5% in November 2013, and the Reserve Bank set itself a path to bring it down: 8% by January 2015, 6% by January 2016.","source":"Speech, 'Seven Ages of India's Monetary Policy', Shri Shaktikanta Das, Governor, RBI, para 16, 24-01-2020","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/commonperson/english/Scripts/speeches.aspx?Id=3161"},{"chart":"rel","month":"2016-08","label":"A 4% inflation target","note":"After the RBI Act was amended in May 2016, the Government notified a 4% consumer price inflation target on 05-08-2016, with a band of 2% to 6%. A six-member Monetary Policy Committee was set up on 29-09-2016. Inflation has since run closer to partners'.","source":"RBI, 'Monetary Policy' overview, section 'Monetary Policy Framework'","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/FS_Overview.aspx?fn=2752"},{"chart":"rel","month":"2022-04","label":"Inflation peaks at 7.8%","note":"Consumer price inflation in India peaked at 7.8% in April 2022, on supply disruptions, higher world commodity prices after the war in Ukraine, and the currency; it stayed above 6% from January to October 2022.","source":"RBI Annual Report 2022-23, Chapter II: Economic Review, para II.3.3, 30-05-2023","url":"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/AnnualReportPublications.aspx?Id=1373"}];
